COMMAND NAME: gpcreatepart

Partitions a table by creating inherited child tables, defining 
check constraints, creating rewrite rules, setting table permissions, 
and moving existing data from the parent table into the correct 
child table.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpcreatepart -t <schema>.<parent_table> -f <partition_column> -p d 
-c {y|m|d|h} -s <YYYY-MM-DD> -n <number_children> [-m <conversion>] 
[-r] [-b] [-u] [-i <ixcolumn1>[,<ixcolumn2>,...]] 
[-A {<distcolumn1>[,<distcolumn2>,...] | 'DISTRIBUTED RANDOMLY'}] 
[-F] [-N] [-C] [-B <parallel_processes>] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] 
[-D] [-k] [-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpcreatepart -t <schema>.<parent_table> -f <partition_column> -p r 
-s <range_start_value> -e <range_end_value> -g <range_increment> 
[-m <conversion>] [-r] [-b] [-u] [-i <ixcolumn1>[,<ixcolumn2>,...]] 
[-A {<distcolumn1>[,<distcolumn2>,...] | 'DISTRIBUTED RANDOMLY'}] 
[-F] [-N] [-C] [-B <parallel_processes>] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] 
[-D] [-k] [-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpcreatepart -t <schema>.<parent_table> -f <partition_column> -p l 
-o <list_value1>,<list_value2>[,<list_value3>,...] [-m <conversion>] 
[-r] [-b] [-u] [-i <ixcolumn1>[,<ixcolumn2>,...]] 
[-A {<distcolumn1>[,<distcolumn2>,...] | 'DISTRIBUTED RANDOMLY'}] 
[-F] [-N] [-C] [-B <parallel_processes>] [-q] [-a] [-l <log_dir>] 
[-D] [-k] [-d <master_data_directory>] [-x <database>]

gpcreatepart -? | -v


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpcreatepart utility automates the administrative tasks of 
partitioning a large parent table into several smaller child tables. 
It performs the following tasks:

* Creates child tables which inherit attributes from the parent 
  table (columns, constraints, distribution key, table permissions). 
  Note that you can optionally define a different Greenplum 
  distribution policy on the child tables using the -A option. 
  The child table names are created using a naming convention of: 
    <top_level_parent>_c_d_<child_depth_#>_<partition_type>_
    <partition_number>_<oid_of_parent_table>_<partition_criteria>
   
    For example:
    sales_c_d_1_m_6_19555_month

* Defines CHECK constraints on the child tables based on the 
  date range, numeric range, or list value specified. 

* Creates the appropriate rewrite rules on the parent table 
  so that INSERT statements are automatically redirected to 
  the correct child table. The rewrite rules are created using 
  a naming convention of: 
    rule_<child_table_name>

    For example:
    rule_sales_c_d_1_m_6_19555_month

* If -i is supplied, creates the specified indexes on the 
  child tables. Indexes are created with a naming convention of:
     idx_<column_number>_<child_table_name>

     For example:
     idx_1_sales_c_d_1_m_6_19555_month

* If the parent table contains data, removes partitioned records 
  from the parent table into a temporary table and re-inserts 
  them into parent table. The rewrite rules then route the rows 
  to the correct child table based on the CHECK constraints. You 
  can optionally use the -C option to insert rows into the child 
  tables directly.

Table partitioning addresses the problem of supporting very large 
tables, such as fact tables, by allowing you to divide them into 
smaller and more manageable pieces. Partitioning is especially 
useful in data warehouse applications, which commonly store and 
analyze large amounts of historical data.

In Greenplum Database, partitioning is a procedure that creates 
multiple sub-tables (or child tables) from a single large table 
(or parent table). The primary purpose is to improve performance 
by scanning only the relevant data needed to satisfy a query. 
This is done using table inheritance and CHECK constraints. Table 
inheritance creates a persistent relationship between a child table 
and its parent table(s), so that all of the schema information 
from the parent table propagates to its children. By default, 
Greenplum Database scans all of the data in a parent table and 
all of its child tables to satisfy a given query. CHECK constraints 
limit the data a table can contain based on some defining criteria, 
and are also used by the query planner to determine which child 
tables to scan in order to satisfy a given query. 

The way you choose to partition a table depends upon how you plan 
to access the data, and your performance requirements. You should 
also try to choose partitioning criteria that will divide your data 
as evenly as possible. A given query’s performance improves based 
on the number of partitions created. For example, by dividing a 
large table into 10 partitions, a query will execute 10 times 
faster than it would against the unpartitioned table (provided 
that the data is equally distributed and that the partitions 
are designed to support the query’s criteria).


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-t <schema>.<parent_table>

Required. The name of the parent table to partition. 
Table names must be qualified with the schema name 
in which the table resides.


-f <partition_column>

Required. The name of the column that will be evaluated 
in order to partition the parent table.


-p d | r | l (date, range, or list partitions)

Required. Specifies the partitioning type. Greenplum Database 
supports date range partitioning (based on date, timestamp, 
or timestamp with timezone data), numeric range partitioning 
(based on numeric data), or list partitioning (based on 
character data).


-c y | m | d | h (date range basis)

Required for date range partitioning. Specifies if 
partitions should be created on a yearly, monthly, 
daily, or hourly basis.


-s <range_start_value>

Required for date and numeric range partitioning. For date 
partitioning, specifies the start date to use as the partitioning 
starting point in the format YYYY-MM-DD hh:mm:ss. For numeric 
range partitioning, specifies the numeric value to use as 
the partitioning starting point.


-n <number_of_children> (date range limit)

Required for date range partitioning. Specifies the number 
of year, month, day, or hour child tables to create from 
the starting point.


-e <range_end_value> 

Required for numeric range partitioning. Specifies the 
range ending value for the data contained in the partition 
column.


-g <range_increment>

Required for numeric range partitioning. Specifies how to 
increment the records between the starting range and 
ending range into child tables.


-o <list_values>

Required for list partitioning. A comma separated list 
of values for which to create child tables. Make sure there 
are no spaces between list values and comma separators. To 
add a null check constraint add the text NULL to the list.


-m <conversion>

Optional. The conversion function to used to convert the 
partition column data type format to the format you want 
to use to evaluate CHECK constraints and rewrite rules. 
For example, if a numeric column contains timestamp data 
in the format 1138576193.697, supplying -m to_timestamp 
will convert the contents of the column to a timestamp format.


-r (no rewrite rules)

Does not create rewrite rules to redirect INSERT statements on 
the parent table to the correct child table. The default is to 
create INSERT rewrite rules on the parent table.


-b (show child row counts)

Shows the number of records in each child table. This is 
only relevant if the parent table contained data prior to 
being partitioned.


-u (index partition column)

Creates an index on each of the child tables for the 
partition column declared with the -f option.


-i <index_columns>

Creates an index on each of the child tables for the 
specified column(s). The default is to not create indexes 
on the child tables.


-A <distribution_policy>

Specifies a comma-separated list of columns to use as the 
Greenplum distribution key on the child tables. You can also 
specify 'DISTRUBUTED RANDOMLY' to use a random (round-robin) 
distribution policy. If not specified, the child tables will 
use the same distribution policy as the parent table.


-F (force)

Forces child table creation when the parent already has 
child tables.


-N (do not grant table permissions)

Do not grant the table permissions of the parent table 
to the child tables. The default is to grant table 
permissions on the child tables.


-C (move data to child table via direct insert)

Moves data from the parent table into the newly created 
child tables by moving data from the parent table into a 
temporary table and then inserting directly into the child 
tables based on the CHECK constraints defined. The default 
behavior is to insert through the parent table using rewrite 
rules to move the data. This option offers better performance 
than the default behavior, but should only be used if every record 
can be mapped to a child table CHECK constraint. The move will 
roll back if there are any leftover records that do not map to 
a child table, leaving all records in the parent table.


-B <parallel_processes>

The number of parallel insert processes to run. The default is 4.


-q (no output)

Do not output messages to the screen. Write messages to log file only.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-l <log_directory>

Specifies an alternative directory to write the log file. By default 
log files are written to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets log output to debug level.


-k (keep temp files)

Keeps temporary files to use for diagnostics.


-x <database_name>

Optional. The name of the database where the parent 
table resides. If not supplied, the script uses the default 
database (which is the most recently created database).


-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master data directory. If not specified, 
the value set for $MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum 
of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Partition the hr.employee table based on salary range. 
By looking at the data, you have determined the lowest salary 
value to be 10000 and the highest to be 400000. You 
want the child tables to be incremented in the ranges of 20000.

gpcreatepart -t hr.employee -f salary -p r -s 10000 -e 400000 -g 20000


Partition the baby.names table based on gender. This will divide 
the table into two child tables, one for males and one for females. 
You also want the child tables to be indexed on the name_id column.

gpcreatepart -t baby.names -f gender -p l -o M,F -i name_id


Partition the sales.sales table based on date range. Create one 
child table per month for the past 12 months of data. The sale_date 
column you are using for partitioning is currently in numeric format, 
so you need to convert it to a timestamp format in order 
to partition it by date.

gpcreatepart -t sales.sales -f sale_date -p d -c m -s 2005-11-01 -n 12 -m to_timestamp


